{
  "dom_id": "step-q116174",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/eeacce39a60a75fd71a77d54f0008f4469395d5e9b69912db8ce9896320b667f.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/dbb1d802854d4046479c69d297fe1807a9ded488409b385a6a2cffbf1196b4c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y({y}^{2}-6y+9)=-y(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-3y=y(y-3).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y=-y(y-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=-{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-9y.$",
